msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:6
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:9
msgid ""
"FFI-compatible and calling-convention-compatible complex types are to be "
"introduced into `core` to ensure synchronity with C primitives."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:11
msgid "Motivation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:14
msgid ""
"The C standard defines the _memory layout_ of a complex number, but not "
"their _calling convention_. This means crates like `num-complex` require "
"workarounds to interface with FFI using `_Complex`, and cannot pass values "
"directly. The addition of complex numbers to Rust as a lang-item ensures a "
"correct calling convention consistent with C on all platforms, thus better "
"allowing C interop."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:17
msgid "In essence, this RFC makes code like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:23
msgid "callable in Rust without indirection:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:26
msgid "\"C\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:34
msgid "using the standard library's FFI-compatible complex numbers."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:36
msgid "Guide-level explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:39
msgid ""
"`Complex<T>` numbers are in core::num and reexported in std::num, like `use "
"core::num::Complex` or `use std::num::Complex`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:40
msgid ""
"`Complex<T>` numbers can be instantiated with any component type using "
"`Complex::new(re, im)` where `re` and `im` are of the same type ( includes "
"all numbers)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:46
msgid "Simple arithmetic is supported:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:51
msgid "// 4 + 6i\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:52
msgid "// -2 - 2i\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:53
msgid "// -5 + 10i\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:54
msgid "// 0.44 - 0.8i\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:57
msgid "Reference-level explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:60
msgid ""
"The `core` crate will provide implementations for operator traits for "
"possible component types. They will have an internal representation similar "
"to this (with public fields for real and imaginary parts):"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:63
msgid ""
"// in core::num::complex, which would be a private module holding complex "
"types\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:64
msgid "\"complex\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:64
msgid "// for calling convention.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:70
msgid "have a constructor"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:78
msgid "and have simple arithmetic implementations supported:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:81 src/3892-complex-numbers.md:82
#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:84 src/3892-complex-numbers.md:85
msgid "/* ... */"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:88
msgid "Drawbacks"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:91
msgid ""
"The multiple emitted calls to `libgcc.so` (`__mulsc3` and the like) via "
"compiler-builtins may cause a bit of overhead and may not be what the Rust "
"lang team and compiler team want."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:93
msgid "Rationale and alternatives"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:96
msgid ""
"The rationale for this type is mostly FFI: C libraries that may be linked "
"from Rust code currently cannot provide functions with direct struct "
"implementations of Complex - they must be hidden under at least a layer of "
"indirection. This is because of the undefined calling convention of complex "
"numbers in C. For example: on powerpc64-linux-gnu, [returning double "
"\\_Complex doesn't do the same thing as returning a struct with a field of "
"type double\\[2\\].](https://gcc.godbolt.org/z/hh7zYcnK6) However, it is not "
"always possible to write a C complex-valued function that wraps the first "
"function in a pointer. Thus, FFI becomes a problem if such complex-valued "
"functions are passed by value and not by reference."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:98
msgid ""
"Additionally, this provides a unified API for complex numbers. Right now, "
"many crates define their own complex types, making interoperability "
"complicated, even though `num-complex` already exports its own type. (`rug::"
"Complex` being an example.) You could theoretically do something like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:107
msgid "for all functions you wish for. But this still needs to happen in C."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:109
msgid "Alternatives:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:111
msgid ""
"Don't do this: There are, obviously, millions of alternatives on crates.io, "
"the foremost being `num-complex`. However, I believe that if we wish to "
"support proper FFI with C, then a standard type that matches calling "
"conventions with C complex numbers is an important feature of the language. "
"Hence, I do not recommend this idea."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:112
msgid ""
"Use a polar layout: Polar complex numbers are undoubtedly a more optimal "
"solution for multiplying complexes. However, I believe that if we wish to "
"have proper FFI with C, then complex number layout should be chosen in "
"accordance with the layout that is used in the C standard, and that is the "
"orthogonal layout. This is also the layout used by most other languages and "
"other crates on crates.io. Additionally, the polar form suffers from many "
"structual issues: it is not a \"natural\" form for expressing complex "
"numbers in computers - you cannot express pi exactly, so you cannot use "
"radians for angle units. Moreover, polar complex numbers do not have a "
"unique representation for each number - it has an infinity of zeros with all "
"possible angles. The final problem, and in my opinion the most fatal, is the "
"complexity of addition:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:114
msgid ""
"$\\left(r_1\\angle\\theta_1\\right) + \\left(r_2\\angle\\theta_2\\right) = "
"\\left(\\sqrt{r_1^2+r_2^2+2r_1r_2\\cos(\\theta_1-"
"\\theta_2)}\\right)\\angle(atan2({r_1\\sin\\theta_1+r_2\\sin\\theta_2},"
"{r_1\\cos\\theta_1+r_2\\cos\\theta_2}))$"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:116
msgid "which offsets any benefits that multiplication may bring."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:118
msgid ""
"Non-generic primitive types: These are, obviously, the most obvious and "
"practical solution. However, if we implemented lots of such types, then we "
"would not be able to expand for `f16` and `f128` support without repeating "
"the code already implemented. It would be extremely repetitive and tedious "
"to document new types and their behavior, even if we used macros to generate "
"implementations."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:119
msgid ""
"Only in `std::ffi`: Many suggestions have been given that `Complex` remains "
"a type in only `std::ffi`. However, these miss a key point of the RFC: this "
"addition is also about creating a unified interface for complex number "
"support in std itself, and making it an FFI type would go against that."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:121
msgid "Prior art"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:124
msgid ""
"FORTRAN, C, C++, Go, Perl and Python all have complex types implemented in "
"the standard library or as a primitive. This clearly appears to be an "
"important feature many languages have. For example, in Python:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:132
msgid "or in C:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:137
msgid "\"%.1f%+.1fi\\n\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:140
msgid "Even in Rust, it has been discussed two times in IRLO:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:142
msgid ""
"[First discussion](https://internals.rust-lang.org/t/c-compatible-complex-"
"types-using-traits/13757)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:143
msgid ""
"[Second discussion](https://internals.rust-lang.org/t/standard-complex-"
"number-in-std-library/23748)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:145
msgid ""
"Many crates, like `num-complex` also provide this feature, though it is not "
"FFI-safe."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:147
msgid "Unresolved questions"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:150
msgid "Future possibilities"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:153
msgid ""
"Maybe later on, we can think of adding a special custom suffix for complex "
"numbers (`1+2j` for example), and using that as a simpler way of writing "
"complex numbers if this RFC is accepted? This is very similar to how most "
"languages implement complex numbers? Or perhaps we could consider a constant:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:161
msgid ""
"where `zero` and `one` is implemented on the `Float` trait similar to "
"`num_traits`? Or maybe we could have a method on normal numbers:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:164
msgid "// for example\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:172
msgid ""
"that could help simplify the life of people who otherwise would have to keep "
"writing `Complex::new()`?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:174
msgid ""
"Arithmetic operations for primitives with complexes? (E.g. `1+Complex::"
"new(0, 2)`). This goes hand in hand with the previous suggestion, so if we "
"choose to implement this we should implement it with the previous suggestion."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:175
msgid ""
"Should we support Imaginary eventually? This RFC doesn't cover it, but I "
"think we can do this later in another RFC."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:176
msgid ""
"Eventually we may support Gaussian integers (an extension of the real "
"integers) which have a Euclidean division procedure with remainder. GCC has "
"these, and we could theoretically eventually support these integers "
"alongside GCC FFI."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:177
msgid "We can also support f16 and f128 once methods for them are stabilised."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:178
msgid ""
"We should also think about a `Display` implementation. Should we support "
"something like `1 + 2i` or something else? Should we not make a `Display` "
"impl at all, and just use re() and im() for the implementation?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:179
msgid ""
"We should also consider adding aliases (like c32 and c64) for floating "
"points once they are established, to allow for a shorthand syntax."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:180
msgid ""
"Eventually, we should also consider adding polar conversions (e.g, `modulus` "
"and `angle`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:181
msgid ""
"And also, we should consider adding complex trig functions (`csin`, `ccos`, "
"etc.) that were deliberately left out of the MVP."
msgstr ""

#: src/3892-complex-numbers.md:182
msgid ""
"Compatibility with other `core::num` types (`NonZero`, `Saturating`, "
"`Wrapping`)."
msgstr ""
